//? Title: Heap permutations: one swap, many copied values
//? Subtitle: Actual recursive swaps, positional duplicates and complete output limits
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Visit every ordering of a small collection while changing only two positions
between visits. Heap's algorithm supplies a deterministic one-swap schedule, but
materializing every full ordering still costs factorial storage and output
copying. This report follows six tiny permutations through actual recursive swap
events, retains the original four-value example, and checks work through eight
positions. Repeated values distinguish positional permutations from unique value
arrangements, and explicit complete limits prevent accidentally requesting an
enormous result. Heap permutations are unrelated to the heap data structure used
by heapsort.
)

\toc

> Six tiny states, five actual swaps

Start with three distinct values 123. The core emits 123, swaps positions 0 and
1 to emit 213, then uses the odd-prefix rule to swap 0 and 2 before
emitting 312. Two more length-two swaps and another odd-prefix swap complete the
six rows. All indices in the API and table below are zero-based.

The trace records the producing prefix size, block counter, swap indices and
values immediately before each actual swap. It does not infer a swap by
comparing output rows or execute the generator again. Ordinary output already
owns every row.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/heap_tiny.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
    std::filesystem::create_directories("build");

    const auto t = dm::algorithms::heap_permutations_trace({1, 2, 3});
    std::print("| next row index | prefix k | completed block i | left | right | left before | right before " "|\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
    for (const auto &e : t.swaps)
        std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n", e.output_index,
                   e.prefix_size, e.block, e.left, e.right, e.left_value,
                   e.right_value);
    std::print("\nRows {}; swaps {}; recursive calls {}; output integer copies {}.\n",
               t.run.work.emissions, t.run.work.swaps,
               t.run.work.recursive_calls, t.run.work.copied_values);
    dm::graphics::Diagram d(80, 1100);
    dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle style;
    style.width = 2.7;
    style.height = 1.5;
    style.font_size = 24;
    const std::vector<dm::graphics::DiagramPoint> positions{{0, 3}, {4.7, 3},
                                                            {9.4, 3}, {9.4, -1},
                                                            {4.7, -1}, {0, -1}};
    for (std::size_t i = 0; i < t.run.permutations.size(); ++i) {
        const auto &v = t.run.permutations[i];
        d.node(std::to_string(i), positions[i],
               std::format("row {}\n{} {} {}", i, v[0], v[1], v[2]), style);
    }
    for (const auto &e : t.swaps)
        d.edge(std::to_string(e.output_index - 1),
               std::to_string(e.output_index),
               std::format("swap {},{}", e.left, e.right));
    d.text({4.7, 5}, "Actual Heap order: one producing swap per edge", 25).save_svg("build/heap_tiny.svg");
    if (t.run.work.swaps != 5 || t.run.work.recursive_calls != 10)
        throw std::runtime_error("tiny counts");
}
