Exact caps retain 6 rows/5 swaps. One-short row/value/history limits reject: true/true/true. Huge count-only request rejects: true.
\image(figures/heap_controls.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Counts retain duplicate rows and actual swaps of equal values. Complete caps separately bound rows, output integers and swap records; none is a byte or peak-memory bound.)
\alttext(Paired bars agree for empty and singleton inputs, then show six emitted rows versus three unique rows for 1, 1, 2 and six versus one for 9, 9, 9.)

`heap_permutations_run` and `heap_permutations_trace` default to one million
rows, one million output integer values and one million history records. Each
zero disables that optional policy. The history cap is ignored without capture.
Exact factorial, product and call-count overflow checks and vector container
limits still apply. All known requirements are checked before the working copy,
output allocation or first swap. Exceeding a cap returns no truncated or partial
result; allocation failure can still occur despite representable admitted
counts.

Legacy `heap_permutations` retains its original signature, order and
repeated-value behavior, with no optional policy caps. It now rejects
unrepresentable complete counts before copying or recursion. Inputs and all
output rows own independent storage; trace events own their scalar values and
retain no input references. Calls share no mutable state. Selected work excludes
initial input copying, allocation, capture, validation and memory overhead; the
named output-copy count is not total memory traffic.

> What the examples establish

Heap's parity rule achieves one actual transposition between emissions. The
original example preserves all 24 rows, while repeated-value controls explain
why a swap need not visibly change a row. Factorial generation is practical only
for small inputs, and returning every full row introduces an additional n
copying factor. The complete limits expose that cost before execution. No
external dataset is needed for this finite combinatorial task, and these checks
make no runtime comparison with other permutation generators.

The primary reference is B. R. Heap (1963), [Permutations by
Interchanges](https://doi.org/10.1093/comjnl/6.3.293), The Computer Journal 6.
The paper's second program uses the parity-dependent partner rule without
storing skeleton interchange lists; the library expresses that schedule
recursively and materializes every row.

Verification compares the entire legacy order with a frozen implementation,
checks exact event-to-row transitions and selected work under sanitizers, and
compares unique output sets with independent lexicographic enumeration.
Repeated-value, ownership, count-overflow and exact-cap controls accompany
native and independent oracle checks. Every API example, complete program, local
figure and both exports is verified before retiring the original report.
